<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Coverage is measured over Platformer.Core only, and the CI floor is scoped to
  it.

  Platformer.Desktop is deliberately excluded. It is a thin adapter over Raylib:
  a window, a render loop and key bindings. It cannot run headless, so it cannot
  be exercised in CI, and including it would mean the coverage number stopped
  describing anything real - it would drift down as the renderer grew and up as
  it shrank, regardless of how well the game was tested. The simulation is the
  part of this project that has to be correct, and it is headless by design, so
  it is the part worth putting a floor under.

  The Include filter is an exact assembly-name match, and deliberately so. If the
  simulation is ever split across a second assembly, that assembly is silently
  unmeasured until it is added here - a real assumption, recorded so it is not a
  surprise. Widening to [Platformer.Core*]* would NOT be the fix: it would also
  match Platformer.Core.Tests and start measuring the test assembly against its
  own floor, which inflates the number with code that is tautologically covered.
  Prefer adding the new assembly by name.

  This is not a licence to hide logic in Platformer.Desktop. Anything with
  behaviour worth testing - see the camera in #10, which is required to be a
  testable type with no Raylib dependency - belongs in a type that tests can
  reach, and it will be measured there.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <DataCollectionRunSettings>
    <DataCollectors>
      <DataCollector friendlyName="XPlat code coverage">
        <Configuration>
          <Format>cobertura</Format>
          <Include>[Platformer.Core]*</Include>
          <ExcludeByAttribute>GeneratedCodeAttribute,CompilerGeneratedAttribute,ExcludeFromCodeCoverageAttribute</ExcludeByAttribute>
          <SkipAutoProps>true</SkipAutoProps>
        </Configuration>
      </DataCollector>
    </DataCollectors>
  </DataCollectionRunSettings>
</RunSettings>
